WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:25.119
Charles Ponzi: Natural Born Swindler

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:29.830
Charles Ponzi allegedly once said “I went
looking for trouble, and I found it.”

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:34.239
Truer words were never spoken. From an early
age, Ponzi discovered that hard work and book

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:40.140
learning were not for him. He was always involved
in one scheme after another, hoping that some

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.379
day, he will find the one that would make
him rich beyond his wildest dreams.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:51.289
He turned out to be right. One day, Charles
Ponzi was struck by a lightning bolt of perfidious

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:57.940
inspiration and set in motion one of the most
brazen and ambitious cons in history. The

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.950
basic concept behind the swindle even took
his name. Sure, people like Lou Pearlman and

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:09.490
Bernie Madoff employed the fraud to far greater
success and, as we will find out, he wasn’t

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:15.340
even the first to do it, but, even today,
the con is remembered as the Ponzi scheme.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.570
The con man lured his victims with the simple,
but irresistible promise of quick and easy

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:25.710
money. But this was one temptation that Ponzi
himself could not control. He kept on scheming

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:31.909
and plotting even when his walls of lies were
crashing down around him and the end was inevitable.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:37.500
Ultimately, his deceitful ways cost him everything
- his money, his wife, his friends, his freedom

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.820
and he ended up dying penniless in a charity
hospital in Brazil.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:47.500
Today we look at Charles Ponzi, the con man
who experienced the highest of highs followed

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:55.840
by lowest of lows.
Early Life

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:03.200
Charles Ponzi was born Carlo Pietro Giovanni
Guglielmo Tebaldo Ponzi on March 3, 1882,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.990
in the town of Lugo in northern Italy. His
parents were Oreste and Imelda Ponzi.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.410
A lot of the information on Ponzi’s early
years comes from the man himself from interviews

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.861
he gave after he became a national celebrity,
as well as his boastful autobiography. As

00:02:15.861 --> 00:02:20.520
you might imagine, there is an inherent risk
when you take a con man at his word, but we

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.070
have little choice here. According to Ponzi,
his family was once well-off and lived in

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:29.410
Parma, but had fallen on hard times prior
to his birth. At one point, he relocated to

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:34.500
Rome and took on odd jobs before being accepted
to the Sapienza University of Rome.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.910
By his own admission, Ponzi was not exactly
a conscientious student. Instead, he preferred

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:44.050
to hang around bars and cafes with his rich
friends. As he put it, “spending money seemed

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.950
the most attractive thing on earth.”
Unsurprisingly, the good times did not last

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.200
long and, after a few years, Ponzi was forced
to leave the university with no money and

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.440
no diploma. He heard stories of other Italians
who went off to America to find fame and fortune

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:05.230
and decided that this was the only course
left open for him.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.310
Coming to America
On November 15, 1903, Charles Ponzi arrived

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:18.230
in Boston Harbor aboard the SS Vancouver.
Again, going by his account, he only had $2.50

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.920
in his pockets because he lost the rest of
his money playing cards during the transatlantic

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.400
crossing. However, he had “$1 million in
hopes.”

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.840
Even so, his climb towards the top was long
and slow, and it started with a bunch of odd

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:38.440
jobs up and down the East Coast. He worked
as a busboy, a waiter, a sign painter, but

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:43.220
never lasted in one position long because
he always got in trouble for stealing or trying

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.910
to cheat customers.
After a few years of this, Ponzi moved to

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:53.320
Canada and settled in Montreal. In 1907, he
found a job as an assistant teller with Zarossi

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:58.070
Bank, a new business opened to cater to the
recent influx of Italian immigrants. Here,

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.730
Ponzi managed to rise through the ranks quickly
because he was charming and outgoing and could

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.760
speak multiple languages. He might have been
able to launch a successful career as a banker,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.750
if not for the fact that the owner of the
bank, Luigi Zarossi, was operating his own

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:18.150
scam. He attracted new clients with generous
interest rates on bank deposits, except that

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:23.230
he was paying on those interests using the
money deposited into new accounts. Meanwhile,

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:27.700
he used the profits for real estate loans.
Eventually, the scheme failed and Zarossi

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:34.820
fled to Mexico. Ponzi was, once again, penniless.
He forged a check for $423.58 and got caught.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:40.730
He ended up serving three years in St. Vincent-de-Paul
Federal Penitentiary in Montreal before returning

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:44.020
to the United States.
Back in Boston, it did not take long for Ponzi

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.290
to get involved in another criminal endeavor.
This time, it was smuggling Italian immigrants

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:52.400
across the border. He was caught again and
spent another two years in prison.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.650
After becoming a free man again, it seems
that Ponzi tried to walk the straight and

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.400
narrow, at least for a little while. Perhaps
part of the reason was Rose Gnecco, a young

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.330
woman he met on the streetcar one day and
fell in love with. Ponzi wooed her endlessly

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:10.210
and, soon enough, the innocent girl was enamored
with his charm and apparent sophistication.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:15.070
The two married in 1918.
During that time, Ponzi first worked as a

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:20.530
teller for broker J. R. Poole, and afterwards
left to take over his father-in-law’s grocery

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:24.880
store. Neither endeavor proved successful
and, soon enough, Charles Ponzi was looking

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:33.140
for a new get-rich-quick-scheme.
The Ponzi Scheme

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.880
Even if you never heard of Charles Ponzi before
this video, chances are that you were still

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.970
familiar with the scam that shares his name
- the Ponzi scheme. But what exactly does

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.970
it involve?
A Ponzi scheme is an investment fraud that

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:51.410
relies on the old adage “rob Peter to pay
Paul.” The basics of the con involve paying

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:55.660
off the “dividends” of older investors
with money from new investors.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:06:00.090
The people think their money gets invested
wisely in profitable business ventures and

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:05.200
that the person in charge is just really good
at their job. Most of the funds, however,

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.070
go straight into the pocket of the schemer.
In exchange, the victims get some money back

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:14.571
which, in their minds, represents the profits
they made from legitimate transactions. As

00:06:14.571 --> 00:06:19.980
far as they are concerned, the bulk of their
investment is still safe and sound, not realizing

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:25.139
that it is already gone, used to pay off other
investors and to finance a luxurious lifestyle

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.340
for the con artist.
The biggest drawback of the Ponzi scheme is

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:33.680
that it requires a constant flow of new investors
to pay off the earlier ones. If the well runs

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.330
dry, the scam falls apart. That is why these
swindlers do their best to ensure a steady

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:44.580
supply of new funds by promising huge profits
with no risks. This turns the scam into a

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.900
bit of a vicious circle: the more “profits”
the con men pay out, the more new investors

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.760
they need which, in turn, raises the amount
of “profits” being paid out, which results

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.020
in the need for new investors and so on.
Once a Ponzi scheme is set in motion, it doesn’t

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:05.040
really stop until it crashes and burns and
the fraud is exposed. Even so, a skillful

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.170
con man can keep it going for years, even
decades. There is usually no shortage of people

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.440
unable to resist the allure of fast and easy
money.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.950
The Ponzi scheme gets generally mixed up with
the pyramid scheme and, although the two are

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:23.639
very similar, there are a few key differences.
Most importantly, in a Ponzi scheme, the fraudster

00:07:23.639 --> 00:07:29.910
acts as sole operator and interacts with all
his targets directly. In a pyramid scheme,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:34.570
however, participants need to recruit others
into the fray in order to gain any benefits,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:40.150
thus creating a layer of separation between
them and the original fraudster each time.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:45.310
Moreover, a Ponzi scheme promises the victims
profits through obscure methods of investment,

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.840
while pyramid scheme participants know from
the outset that their returns come from signing

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:54.400
up new members. Generally, a Ponzi scheme
is more durable because it requires fewer

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.040
people to stay afloat.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.370
Although Charles Ponzi’s name is inexorably
attached to this type of con, he was not the

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:05.360
first to employ it. A former German actress
named Adele Spitzeder might lay claim to that

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.250
title after opening a bank in 1871 and using
this method to pay off her investors. Her

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.490
scam only lasted a little over a year, but
was soon followed by Austrian Johann Baptist

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:20.790
Placht whose fraud was discovered in 1874.
In America, Sarah Howe might be the first

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:26.080
to employ this technique. In 1879, she opened
a bank named the Ladies’ Deposit Company

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.419
in Boston which offered large interest rates
on deposits. She claimed to have backing from

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:34.940
a Quaker charity, but was simply using money
from new depositors to pay off the interest

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:40.070
rates. Another notable swindler was William
“520 percent” Miller who operated the

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:46.040
Franklin Syndicate. He defrauded $1 million
in 1890s money before being caught.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:49.470
There are some red flags which the United
States Securities and Exchange Commission

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:54.960
or SEC say can help investors avoid Ponzi
schemes. They include the promise of unreasonably

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.820
high returns with no risk, consistent returns
regardless of the state of the market, shady

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:05.270
paperwork, unlicensed sellers, and secretive
or complex investment strategies. If it sounds

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:56.420
too good to be true, it probably is.
How

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:50.060
the Scam Worked
Now we have a general idea of what a Ponzi

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:55.980
scheme is, but how exactly did Charles Ponzi’s
specific scam work? It was based around a

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:02.770
special postal coupon known as an International
reply coupon or IRC. Introduced in 1906, it

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:08.080
was meant to be exchanged for the postage
stamp necessary to send one single-rate, ordinary

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.240
delivery between two countries which were
members of the Universal Postal Union (UNU).

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.950
In other words, a person from the United States,
let’s call him Jack, sent a letter to Marie

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:23.210
in France. He wanted a reply, but was not
expecting Marie to spend her own money on

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:27.660
postage stamps to write him back. Therefore,
he also included with the original letter

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.720
an IRC which he bought in the U.S. which Marie
could redeem in her own country for the postage

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.460
necessary to send back another letter.
Ponzi had never heard of these coupons until

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.520
he received one from a company in Spain. He
had written many letters to businesses in

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.390
Europe presenting various ideas of his and
this company wrote him back and included an

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:51.260
IRC for his reply. Whatever money-making pitch
Ponzi originally made to the Spanish company

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.290
was quickly forgotten as he came up with a
new idea.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:59.080
Ponzi realized that inflation, mainly caused
by World War I, meant there was a slight,

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:03.800
but noticeable difference in the value of
the stamps which were exchanged using IRCs.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.660
The redemption rate of these coupons was fixed
through an international treaty, but one could,

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:13.500
theoretically, make a profit if they bought
IRCs in a different country using currency

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:18.140
that had fallen against the dollar, then exchanging
them for stamps in the United States and then

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:23.209
selling those stamps in U.S. currency.
Ponzi could, for example, buy an IRC in a

00:12:23.209 --> 00:12:28.610
Spanish post office worth 30 centavos. He
could then exchange it in the U.S. for a stamp

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:33.870
worth 5 cents and make a 10 percent profit.
If he scaled up this operation, he could earn

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:39.080
substantial dividends. The profit would be
even greater if he bought IRCs from countries

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:45.770
with the highest inflation.
The Securities Exchange Company

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:51.000
Basically, what Ponzi wanted was a type of
arbitrage, which simply means purchasing something

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:55.390
in one market and then selling it in another
market where the price is higher. This was

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:58.970
not only common and profitable, but also perfectly
legal.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:03.370
Ponzi had his sales pitch and now he needed
people willing to invest. He made bold claims

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:07.700
of having agents all over Europe who were
buying up IRCs in bulk and sending them back

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:11.600
to him so he could exchange and sell them
for a profit. He boasted that he could provide

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:17.160
returns on investment of 50 percent in 45
days or 100 percent in 90 days. Any request

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:21.820
for additional details would have been denied
on the basis that it could help the competition.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:25.720
In reality, setting up such an operation would
have been so costly that it would have completely

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:30.570
negated any profits made from selling IRCs.
Not to mention the fact that there were nowhere

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:35.360
near enough postal coupons in the world to
sustain a business the size that Ponzi’s

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.380
would reach. But these weren’t details that
needed concern his clients.

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:43.750
At first, Ponzi tried the banks, but they
were not biting and refused to grant him loans

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:48.360
for this new business. However, he found considerably
more success simply appealing to the general

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:53.240
population who proved more inclined to believe
his tales of easy riches. At first, he mainly

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:57.490
appealed to friends and acquaintances. Like
his biographer Donald Dunn argued, Ponzi’s

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:02.680
biggest talent had nothing to do with being
good with finances, but being good with people.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:07.250
He made sure not to be too aggressive with
his pitches. He casually flaunted his success

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:11.779
and only went into detail when pressed by
others. Then, if they absolutely insisted,

00:14:11.779 --> 00:14:13.480
he would take their money.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:18.670
In January 1920, Ponzi started the Securities
Exchange Company. By then, the money had begun

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:22.950
rolling in and the swindler had a supply of
investors. Launching his own business was

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:27.410
the natural next step, as well as a necessary
move in order to attract new clients and keep

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:32.100
delivering those promised returns. Soon enough,
he relocated to a bigger office on Boston’s

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:35.800
School Street, the site of the first public
school in the United States.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:43.110
Let the Good Times Roll
It wasn’t long before Ponzi was handling

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:47.310
millions of dollars from tens of thousands
of investors. People were lining up around

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:51.540
the block to give their money to this financial
wizard who came out of nowhere and had the

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:57.990
secret to untold wealth. At the height of
his success, Ponzi was making around $250,000

00:14:57.990 --> 00:14:58.990
a day.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:02.910
Obviously, this newfound success allowed him
to indulge in a lavish lifestyle. He bought

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:08.430
a spacious 12-room mansion in Lexington, Massachusetts.
He rode around town in a custom-built chauffeured

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:12.940
limousine, wearing expensive tailored suits
with diamond tie pins and carrying around

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:17.560
a gold-handled cane. Some of his more outlandish
expenditures included buying a bank which

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:21.899
previously rejected his loan application,
as well as Poole’s brokerage firm where

00:15:21.899 --> 00:15:25.160
he used to work just so he could fire his
former boss.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:29.440
The good times lasted less than a year for
Ponzi before he got caught and, looking back,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:33.920
it is surprising that it even took that long.
He might have been an incredible salesman,

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:38.880
but Ponzi was not a skilled financier like
Bernie Madoff, for example, who could keep

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:44.170
his scam going for decades. In fact, Ponzi’s
own publicist later called him a “financial

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:48.560
idiot” who couldn’t add.
His scheme did not stand up to scrutiny from

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:53.730
a person who knew what they were talking about.
However, Ponzi managed to delay investigations,

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:58.459
first by successfully suing a financial writer
for libel which acted as a deterrent to other

00:15:58.459 --> 00:16:03.529
journalists, and then by sweet talking state
officials and easing their growing suspicions.

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:08.029
Ponzi’s world came tumbling down in the
summer of 1920, mostly courtesy of the Boston

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:12.829
Post which even won the Pulitzer Prize for
Public Service for their exposure of Ponzi’s

00:16:12.829 --> 00:16:17.130
operations. However, credit is also due to
William McMasters, the man Ponzi hired as

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:22.870
his publicist shortly before his downfall.
Exposed

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:29.301
At first, the relationship between the three
started off great. One of McMasters’ first

00:16:29.301 --> 00:16:33.220
acts as Ponzi’s publicist was to schedule
him an interview with the Boston Post which,

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:38.100
at the time, had the largest circulation in
New England. On July 24, a piece on Ponzi

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:42.519
appeared on the newspaper’s front page.
It detailed his rags-to-riches story and how

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:47.230
he was able to provide all of his investors
with amazing returns that no other financier

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:52.110
could come close to. On July 26, a Monday,
Ponzi went to his office to find a massive

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:57.540
line of people waiting to give him their money.
In just that one day, Ponzi collected over

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:02.570
$3 million which is over $34 million in modern
currency.

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:07.819
This huge success came with drawbacks as it
made Ponzi front-page news all over the country.

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:12.329
The Boston Post, in particular, now had a
strong interest in him. Concurrently, McMasters

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:16.370
began doubting his client’s claims, realizing
that it would have been impossible for anyone

00:17:16.370 --> 00:17:19.329
to deliver the type of returns that Ponzi
promised.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:23.639
Eventually, McMasters became convinced that
his client was a fraud and began working with

00:17:23.639 --> 00:17:27.970
the Post to expose him. The newspaper’s
acting publisher, Richard Grozier, was initially

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:32.889
wary of the idea because he feared a lawsuit,
but McMasters got him onboard with the help

00:17:32.889 --> 00:17:38.149
of district attorney Nathan Tufts who promised
him immunity from lawsuits if the exposé

00:17:38.149 --> 00:17:39.149
proved false.

00:17:39.149 --> 00:17:44.009
On August 2, the Boston Post ran a first-person
account written by McMasters which had the

00:17:44.009 --> 00:17:49.009
headline “Declares Ponzi Is Now Hopelessly
Insolvent.” The publicist proclaimed that

00:17:49.009 --> 00:17:53.600
Ponzi was at least $2 million in debt and
up to $4.5 million if you took into account

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:58.210
the interest on his outstanding notes.
The downfall came quick for Ponzi. He tried

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:02.462
reassuring his investors and lashing out at
his accusers, but there was no way for him

00:18:02.462 --> 00:18:06.830
to stop government auditors from looking at
his books. On August 11, the Post further

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:11.570
hurried his demise by revealing that Ponzi
spent time in prison in Canada for forging

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:15.899
checks. An official report from the U.S. Post
Office revealed that Ponzi nevered cashed

00:18:15.899 --> 00:18:21.559
in any IRCs in the United States, while an
audit concluded that he was $3 million (later

00:18:21.559 --> 00:18:26.039
revised to $7 million) in debt.
Charles Ponzi was arrested on federal charges

00:18:26.039 --> 00:18:31.129
of mail fraud. His downfall led to the collapse
of six banks and caused the financial ruin

00:18:31.129 --> 00:18:36.460
of tens of thousands of people who received
less than 30 cents on the dollar on their

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:41.879
investments.
The Scams Continue

00:18:41.879 --> 00:18:47.000
Initially, Ponzi only served three-and-a-half
years in prison before being released on parole.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:51.090
However, he was immediately arrested again
on state charges of larceny and sentenced

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.799
to another seven to nine years in prison.
Ponzi appealed the state conviction and was

00:18:54.799 --> 00:18:59.210
released on bail until the matter was settled.
He immediately fled Massachusetts and ended

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:03.519
up in Jacksonville, Florida. Despite the complete
collapse of his financial house of cards,

00:19:03.519 --> 00:19:08.129
Ponzi had no intention of turning straight.
As he said in his autobiography, “no man

00:19:08.129 --> 00:19:13.350
is ever licked, unless he wants to be. And
I didn’t intend to stay licked.”

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:16.830
He immediately got started on another scam
which meant to take advantage of the real

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:21.899
estate boom in Florida. He founded the Charpon
Land Syndicate and sold property to oblivious

00:19:21.899 --> 00:19:26.590
investors who were lured in by Ponzi’s charm
and his promises of huge returns on valuable

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:30.529
tracts of land in just a few months. What
they soon discovered was that they actually

00:19:30.529 --> 00:19:35.029
purchased worthless swampland and property
that was underwater.

00:19:35.029 --> 00:19:38.600
Ponzi was arrested again. However, before
the authorities fully realized who they were

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:43.359
dealing with, he posted bail and went on the
run once more. This was one too many close

00:19:43.359 --> 00:19:47.789
calls for the con man and he decided to escape
to Italy. He shaved his head and mustache

00:19:47.789 --> 00:19:52.350
and secured passage as a crewman aboard a
merchant ship headed for Europe. However,

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:56.940
his identity was discovered and Ponzi was
arrested in the port of New Orleans.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:03.840
Final Years
This time, there was no more escaping for

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.929
the supreme swindler. He served seven years
in prison, was released in 1934 and then deported

00:20:08.929 --> 00:20:12.549
to Italy because Ponzi never actually obtained
his American citizenship.

00:20:12.549 --> 00:20:16.779
The final years of his life are not well-documented
because they were not covered in his autobiography

00:20:16.779 --> 00:20:20.779
and, also, because the world had lost interest
in Charles Ponzi by then. The man who left

00:20:20.779 --> 00:20:25.440
prison was not the same as the one who entered
it. Ponzi had lost his youth, his confidence,

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:29.909
and his guile. His wife did not follow him
to Italy and divorced him a few years later.

00:20:29.909 --> 00:20:33.730
Ponzi didn’t last long in his motherland
and ended up in Brazil. According to one version

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:37.179
of events, this was because he got caught
conning people again, and had to go on the

00:20:37.179 --> 00:20:41.130
run. Another version said that a cousin of
his who worked in the air force got him a

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.900
job with an airline that ran flights between
Italy and Brazil. The gig only lasted for

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:48.980
a few years, though, as the fledgling airline
was closed when Brazil sided with the Allies

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:51.729
during World War II.
Charles Ponzi spent the last few years of

00:20:51.729 --> 00:20:56.100
his life in squalor, working as a teacher
and translator to make ends meet. A stroke

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:01.419
left him partially blind and paralyzed. He
died in January 18, 1949, in a charity hospital

00:21:01.419 --> 00:21:20.820
in Rio de Janeiro, barely leaving behind the
money needed to cover

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:22.610
his burial.

